WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.350 align:start position:0%
 
i'm really happy that we're going to be  able to have time to talk with  uh film editor andrew marcus uh andrew's  career  spans over three decades his films  include howard's end american psycho  under the tuscan sun dirty dancing sing  street  isn't it romantic and of course the film  we're celebrating today  hedwig and the angry inch for its 20th  anniversary  so andrew let's talk about hedwig  i'm super excited i love hedwig and i  love every opportunity to talk about  it fantastic so i think the first thing  we'd like to know is  uh and i think uh kind of like  actors there's probably somewhat of a  casting process  for editors where people are looking for  a  certain aesthetic or whatever so tell us  how you came on board and how you came  to be chosen for this project  well i have a very circuitous route to  hedwig i um  i was doing period movies in london  all the marching ivory and kenneth  branam movies  and i really wanted to break out of that  world and do  more edgy projects so i started working  with a company called muse productions  in los angeles and they did trees lounge  two girls and a guy virgin suicides  and we were looking for projects  to produce and i had seen hedwig  at squeezebox and then i had seen it at  westbath  and when it opened at jane street i  brought all the producers  amused and some of their investors to a  number of productions  i was really excited about trying to  bring it to the film  unfortunately jersey productions  danny devito's company was also involved  and we  got out of the running really quickly  but then  when i was editing american psycho  we were editing in the apartment complex  that christine vashon lived in  and my husband was working for marlene  christine's  wife and she just casually mentioned  that she was going to produce hedwig and  i  literally stalked her every day to get  the job  so and i did eventually that's a great  story  and i love again the idea of you going  from uh merchant ivory into  into hedwig and american psycho so you  you definitely got to make your  transition congratulations that's not  always easy to do  what were some of the biggest challenges  that you faced with hedwig and  and you know it's such a wide variety  of styles and and you know  types of things going on you know  there's the stage shows there's the  the you know animation i mean it's a  really  visual uh diverse film and how did you  make the editing style all fit together  uh  and what what were your uh you know  challenges as you well began  combining all of this i mean to john's  credit  he had workshopped it at sundance for  a year before we began and  he storyboarded it and he's an  incredible student of cinema  and so he had a lot of a lot of the  challenges of adapting it to film he had  worked out before i came  on there weren't a lot of challenges  filmically the real challenges were  on stage john was tommynosis  and hedwig and then when we had  another actor playing tommynosis that in  the end  it's very easy if you're the same  character to merge into the same person  but i think their biggest challenge was  creating an ending that was moving  where they became one  as in the origin of love story but they  were two separate  actors and that took a long time to work  out and it took a lot of  sort of re-editing and rethinking how  the ending was working  what about the animation part of it what  was the challenge in that and how was  that accomplished  i would say that wasn't a challenge  working with emily hubley was one of the  greatest experiences of my career she's  a complete genius and because  the animations were roughly projected  while we were shooting it we had a lot  of the animations  as stills before we even began shooting  so we had it roughed out and  it was sort of an embarrassment of  riches the real challenge was how do you  cut away from a great performance  to animation and where do you want to be  at any one time because to tell like the  story of origin of love you really need  the animations  but you also want to hear john singing  it  and the band playing it so that was the  real challenge finding that balance  we noticed that you also have a credit  for second unit director  and i'm not sure that that's always the  case with film editors how did that come  about  i was a second unit director in a lot of  the marching ivy movies and on kenneth  branagh's movies  and on psycho so it's really easy for  another  to do second unit because you've cut the  sequence  and you know exactly what pieces you  need to piece in  it makes sense for others to do it i'm  surprised more others don't do it  but um i love doing it things like  the gummy bears and all the insert shots  the like  the candy going to luther the gummy  bears the like  bathtub scene and that sort of thing so  you work with headwood  and the gummy bears that is true  dexterity i'm very impressed  it was a real challenge getting the  gummy bears to steam up  that's fantastic thank you so i assume  that you were uh  you know brought on to the film you know  even as it was in production were you on  the set first the shoot  i was actually cutting in a winnebago on  set at all times and  it was such a great experience because i  was  running to the camera when they would  ask questions do we need this shot do we  need that shot people could come to the  winnebago and see a sequence as we were  going  and because of that there was a real  sense of camaraderie that you don't get  any more really on  film sets we all were staying in the  same place we all spent weekends  together  there's a whole sequence of when  hedwig teaches tommy goes over his  record collection  and um that was shot on a saturday in  my the house i was staying in we just  did sort of some gorilla film making  fantastic i i get that sense of uh  camaraderie  and i i i know that that's something  that uh you don't experience on every  film and and  the ones you do i think people really  cherish even all the more just because  of  uh you know the the fun you have and the  family that you build so we were all  great friends  for a really long time and still can see  each other and talk  that's great so uh what are some you  know as an editor you end up watching  uh the film you know multiple times so  what are some of the favorite moments  that you still get a big kick out of as  you're  thinking about hedwig i think the most  successful transformation from stage  to film is wig in a box it's sort of a  perfect  film moment where you know i was reading  i was did isn't it when i did isn't it  romantic we talked about  favorite makeover scenes and there are  lists  all over the internet about favorite  makeover scenes and  i think wig in a box and clueless come  up as the two favorite makeover scenes  i think arie anne's costumes the  production design  the camera everything sort of came  together in that scene and made it  really special and really cinematic and  really kind of thrilling for the  audience  so you mentioned production design you  have any memories of uh  working with therese de prez or any uh  reflections on her work  seeing it as an editor and how it worked  for you i  loved working with therese she taught me  so much about  production design and the value of  production design  when we did bilgewater's which is where  hedwig was booked to perform there was a  decommissioned red lobster  outside of toronto and she redecorated  it  to show that it was a different place  every time but one of the things  one day i picked up the menus and  she had written these extensive menus  that were hysterical  everything was a joke on the menu and  nobody you never saw the menu on  camera but that was just her level of  detail  the motto of the restaurant was bilgewa  there's yesterday's seafood at  tomorrow's prices  and you never saw that but it was just  sort of a gift to everybody on set that  you gave us  i i love the idea of you know  that being in your head as you're  performing on the set and  and uh you know sitting there in that  location a decommissioned red lobster i  love  all the all the things that that would  put into your head as an actor  yeah and it's great for extras it's  great for everybody it just adds a whole  nother layer  fantastic so we're in a very unusual  time  very particular time and at the same  time uh  this film at 20 years still has a  resonance and still  has i think messages for us to  be reminded of regularly so tell me  you know how how do you feel this film  still  impacts 20 years later i think the film  was  way ahead of itself and people  didn't quite understand  what john was getting at when he was  making it  i think john was always really clear  that hedwig isn't a drag queen  hedwig is a genderqueer performer  and the whole notion of genderqueer and  gender fluidity  it's now taken root and people accept it  completely but i remember bringing  really like sophisticated people to the  show  i brought someone who was like a part of  andy warhol's factory as a possible  investor and they didn't get it at all  and i think it was just way way way  ahead of its time what it was saying  what it was  trying to achieve i also think one of  the  sad things when it came out was  gay rock and roll wasn't a big thing and  so  i remember somebody saying it was two  rock and roll for the gays and two gay  for the  for the rockers and i think now it's  found its place  completely and it's not shocking at all  i just showed it to my  13 year old twins and they think it's  the greatest thing i've worked on  finally something cool that i've worked  on one  wonderful uh they'll grow to love  howard's end as  as time passes today anyway i do want to  ask you in a sense you brought it up and  in  talking about it just now uh what what  as an editor  what was your um working with what was  working with the music and what were  your impressions of the  uh music aspects of hedwig and how that  fit in  and uh the contribution that that made  to the film  i mean i love working with music i'm a  secret music theater  queen and i just think songs  convey emotion way better than words  and whether it's like dirty dancing  which i worked on  or sing street or i i think  anything is enhanced with music and so  it was a real privilege to work on this  because i think the songs are so amazing  but  um it also gives you a shortcut you  don't have to do a lot of exposition you  don't have to do  when you were asking me about what  favorite scenes i had  i think wig in a box is a favorite  because of  sort of the makeover aspect but even  re-watching it for this  when hedwig puts on uh  gives the wig to miriam in midnight  radio  and then she falls into people i i tear  up at that  still and i think you couldn't get that  level of emotion  in just a dialogue scene it's completely  conveyed by the combination of music and  acting and performance and it takes it  to a whole nother level  well it's really been fun having a look  at hedwig from the insights of the film  editor i'm really always happy to have  as many of the collaborators and  many of the perspectives uh you know  talking about a film and i really  appreciate your taking time with us  for this conversation today thanks so  much andrew  thank you  you

